        <p class=3D"eyebrow">Persistent interview report =C2=B7 001</p>
        <h1>Chiang Mai Lifestyle</h1>
        <div class=3D"hero-copy"><p>The participant is a Chiang Mai residen=
t and event emcee who seeks experiences both for personal enjoyment and to =
explain local culture and tourism to foreign visitors. Temple visits reliab=
ly provide the relaxation, prayer, and photography outcomes they want. By c=
ontrast, a Facebook-promoted Japanese festival promised Japanese culture an=
d activities but felt primarily like a food festival with contemporary perf=
ormances, leading the participant to leave within an hour and regard the vi=
sit as wasted time. That mismatch reduced their trust in event promotion an=
d made them more selective, with attendance around once a month considered =
sufficient. They now reject events using AI-generated promotional images, r=
ead social-media feedback, and sometimes ask a trusted friend to attend fir=
st and report back privately through LINE. Some friends reportedly share th=
e same uncertainty about whether promoted events will match reality. The ce=
ntral decision implication is that the observed need is not broader event d=
iscovery alone, but low-effort, credible evidence that an experience is aut=
hentic and matches its promotion before someone commits limited time.</p></=
div>
      </div>
