    <section class=3D"decision-row">
      <article class=3D"decision"><p class=3D"section-label">Opportunity hy=
pothesis</p><h2>Where the evidence points</h2><p>A bounded hypothesis is th=
at selective, time-constrained event seekers may value a lightweight credib=
ility layer showing recent, real attendee evidence and a clear comparison b=
etween advertised and actual experiences. This could reduce dependence on s=
ending trusted friends first while helping the participant identify cultura=
lly substantive events. The interview supports the behavior and workaround,=
 but does not establish demand for a new product or willingness to contribu=
te, trust, or pay.</p><small>Inference =E2=80=94 validate before treating a=
s fact</small></article>
      <article class=3D"decision next"><p class=3D"section-label">Least-eff=
ort next step</p><h2>What to do next</h2><p>Recruit the participant and fou=
r to six similarly selective Chiang Mai residents from their friend network=
. Show them five real event listings augmented only with recent attendee ph=
otos, a short "promised versus experienced" note, and source identity, then=
 compare their attend-or-skip decisions and confidence with the original Fa=
cebook listings. Useful validation would be repeated decisions made without=
 contacting a friend, accompanied by participants correctly explaining whic=
h evidence changed their confidence; absence of that behavior would weaken =
the hypothesis.</p><small>One useful test, not a roadmap</small></article>
    </section>
